Beryllium: a renderer is sent nothing until the kernel says it has locked down

Purpose: plan/18 section 2, stage B (feature matrix S-STA-01). From
  0.20.3.21 the browser asks the kernel about a renderer when its channel
  connects, and ends one that is not in capability mode. That leaves a
  moment: the invitation went out at launch, so whatever the browser had
  queued for the renderer, and the first navigation can be part of it, was
  there for an unconfined renderer to read before it was ended.
  - content/browser/child_process_launcher_helper.{h,cc}: for a renderer
    launched with the lockdown on, the launcher keeps the Mojo invitation
    back and asks the kernel every 3 ms whether the process is in
    capability mode (KernelSaysCapabilityMode, on the pid fork gave). When
    the answer is yes, the invitation is sent, as it was sent before. A
    renderer needs nothing from the browser to get that far: RendererMain
    locks down before it accepts the invitation. The asking is a delayed
    task on the launcher thread, which stays free for other launches.
    The renderer is ended, never having been sent anything, when it has
    exited, when the kernel gives no answer, when ten seconds have passed,
    or at once when the browser runs with --no-sandbox (under which a
    renderer skips its lockdown). The invitation is destroyed unsent, which
    the browser's side sees as a renderer that went away. A renderer paused
    for a debugger (--renderer-startup-dialog, --wait-for-debugger) is
    waited for without a limit.
    The code that sends the invitation moves, unchanged, into
    SendInvitationOnLauncherThread(), so that both paths use it; the
    launcher's copy of the process handle is made before the send instead
    of after.
  - base/posix/capsicum_rights.{h,cc}: the feature
    BerylliumInviteAfterLockdown, on by default, so that a run can compare
    with the order of 0.20.3.21; ProcessIsGone(pid), true for a process
    that has exited whether or not it has been collected (the kernel still
    answers about the credentials of one that has not).
  Two lines in the log, which is also where the time a renderer takes from
  launch to lockdown is now recorded (plan/18 section 2.3, step 6):
    beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N invited M ms after its launch, in
      capability mode by the kernel's account
    beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N is not in capability mode by the
      kernel's account; ended before it was sent anything, M ms after its
      launch
  The check at connect (beryllium-kernel-attested-renderer.patch) stays as
  it is: with this patch it should never fail, and it does not depend on
  this patch being right.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on beryllium-sender-side-rights.patch
  (the launcher's registration of the confined peer),
  beryllium-kernel-attested-renderer.patch and, for the place in
  base/posix/capsicum_rights, beryllium-kernel-check.patch.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled (written for build 0.20.3.22). base/posix/capsicum_rights.cc as
  patched type-checks whole against FreeBSD 16's headers; the launcher's
  new code type-checks against stand-ins for Chromium's declarations.
  Not verified on a machine: that nothing in a renderer's start before its
  lockdown waits for the browser. If something does, no page loads with
  the lockdown on, and --disable-features=BerylliumInviteAfterLockdown
  shows it at once.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py test_lockdown_log
  requires, under the gate on a build that writes these lines, that every
  renderer the browser attested was invited first, and that the browser
  cut no descriptor for a renderer before that renderer's invitation; it
  records the times from launch to lockdown.
  test_an_unconfined_renderer_is_ended requires that each renderer ended
  under --no-sandbox was ended before it was sent anything.
security-review: required (IPC boundary, process launch)

